{:edb/db-before-t 1316 :edb/db-after-t 1317 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534629 50 #inst "2026-10-08T02:54:54.338Z" 13194139534629 true] [17592186087464 1100 "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/assets/57ec3c089f80e2d35d9b83f6791eefca9b452d933f82209ed434d35d77b78788.png)" 13194139534629 true] [17592186087464 1100 "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/849/Source/Images/89500561af6affb81c697e1bffe36957.png)" 13194139534629 false] [17592186087464 1145 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1\n$$\n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(1,-1)$, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$, and the vertical radius has length $b=3$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - h)^{2})/(a^{2}) + ((y - k)^{2})/(b^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 1)^{2})/(5^{2}) + ((y - (-1))^{2})/(3^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 1)^{2})/(25) + ((y + 1)^{2})/(9) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534629 false] [17592186087464 1145 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(1,-1),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=5,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534629 true] [17592186087467 1100 "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/assets/b6eb2c0222181eaa4eb2a1c232fdf2eee905ab0e0a8a9c2284911ab8dd26cade.png)" 13194139534629 true] [17592186087467 1100 "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/849/Source/Images/0de79119b131777f87a4e2fdd6f3a262.png)" 13194139534629 false] [17592186087467 1145 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1\n$$\n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-1,-3)$, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$, and the vertical radius has length $b=4$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - h)^{2})/(a^{2}) + ((y - k)^{2})/(b^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - (-1))^{2})/(2^{2}) + ((y - (-3))^{2})/(4^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x + 1)^{2})/(4) + ((y + 3)^{2})/(16) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534629 false] [17592186087467 1145 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-1,-3),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-3))^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534629 true] [17592186087468 1100 "For the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{25} + \\dfrac{(y+2)^2}{16} = 1$, find its center, and the lengths of its major and minor axes." 13194139534629 false] [17592186087468 1100 "For the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{25} + \\dfrac{(y+2)^2}{16} = 1,$ find its center, and the lengths of its major and minor axes." 13194139534629 true] [17592186087468 1145 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1\n$$\n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=2$ and $k=-2$, so the center is located at $(2,-2)$.\n\nIn the given equation, we also have $a^2=25$ and $b^2=16$.\n\n- Since $a^2=25$, the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{25} = 5$.\n- Since $b^2=16$, the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16} = 4$.\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=5$, and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4$.\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length\n\n$$\n2a = 2\\cdot5 = 10\n$$\n\nand the minor axis has length\n\n$$\n2b = 2\\cdot4 = 8\n$$" 13194139534629 false] [17592186087468 1145 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=2$ and $k=-2,$ so the center is located at $(2,-2).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have $a^2=25$ and $b^2=16.$\n\n- Since $a^2=25,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{25} = 5.$\n\n- Since $b^2=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16} = 4.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=5,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a = 2\\cdot5 = 10,$ and the minor axis has length $2b = 2\\cdot4 = 8.$" 13194139534629 true] [17592186087771 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299766 13194139534629 true] [17592186087771 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299783 13194139534629 true] [17592186087771 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299786 13194139534629 true] [17592186087771 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299790 13194139534629 true] [17592186087772 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299759 13194139534629 true] [17592186087772 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299769 13194139534629 true] [17592186087772 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299773 13194139534629 true] [17592186087773 1020 #edb/ref 17592186299778 13194139534629 true] [17592186087980 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534629 true] [17592186087980 1100 "What is the center of the ellipse $((x - 1)^{2})/(8) + ((y + 2)^{2})/(4) = 1$?" 13194139534629 false] [17592186087980 1100 "What is the center of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=1?$" 13194139534629 true] [17592186087980 1145 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=1$ and $k=-2,$ so the center is located at $(1,-2).$" 13194139534629 true] [17592186087981 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534629 true] [17592186087981 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/assets/92a107b34d3ed8c422b35dc33eec5f54a47bc0db054f5a6c61b390d996d8bec4.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139534629 true] [17592186087981 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/849/Source/Images/q-16555.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139534629 false] [17592186087981 1145 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(3,-1),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=5,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=1.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{25}+(y+1)^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534629 true] [175921